<h3>Detecting MSG/DSG</h3>
<p>Currently, there is no consumer-friendly test kit to detect MSG/DSG in food. Laboratory methods include:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Mass Spectrometry (LC-MS/MS)</strong> - Gold standard for accurate quantification <sup><a href="#src-detect">detect</a>, <a href="#src-detect-2">detect-2</a>, <a href="#src-detect-3">detect-3</a>, <a href="#src-detect-4">detect-4</a>, <a href="#src-detect-4-rep1">detect-4-rep1</a>, <a href="#src-detect-4-rep2">detect-4-rep2</a>, <a href="#src-detect-4-rep3">detect-4-rep3</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>HPLC</strong> - High-precision measurement used in food testing labs <sup><a href="#src-detect">detect</a>, <a href="#src-detect-2">detect-2</a></sup></li>
<li><strong>Enzymatic Assay Kits</strong> - Moderate cost, requires spectrophotometer</li>
<li><strong>Test Strips</strong> - Detect L-Glutamate (MSG) via enzymatic reaction; semi-quantitative (0-300 mg/L), useful for screening but not precise <sup><a href="#src-teststrips">teststrips</a>, <a href="#src-teststrips-2">teststrips-2</a>, <a href="#src-teststrips-3">teststrips-3</a>, <a href="#src-teststrips-4">teststrips-4</a></sup></li>
</ul>

<h3>Practical Tips</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read the label</strong> - Always check ingredient lists for MSG (monosodium glutamate, E621), DSG (disodium glutamate, E622), and hidden names (yeast extract, hydrolyzed protein, etc.)</li>
<li><strong>Ask the restaurant</strong> - Politely ask if they use MSG in their food. Note: they may not know or may refuse to answer.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid breaded foods</strong> - At fast-food restaurants, avoid anything with breading (fried chicken, fish sandwiches) as these often contain MSG</li>
<li><strong>Avoid "fire hot" or "extreme flavor"</strong> - Products claiming extra spicy or intense flavor often use MSG to enhance taste</li>
<li><strong>MSG = profit move</strong> - MSG allows use of heavily processed ingredients while maintaining flavor. It's used to mask lower-quality inputs.</li>
<li><strong>Umami without MSG</strong> - Real chefs get umami naturally from aged cheeses, mushrooms, soy sauce, tomatoes, anchovies. No need to stoop to MSG.</li>
<li><strong>Umami comes from glutamate</strong> - The "savory" flavor comes from glutamate, not from salt. MSG = salt + glutamate <sup><a href="#src-umami">umami</a>, <a href="#src-umami-2">umami-2</a>, <a href="#src-umami-3">umami-3</a>, <a href="#src-umami-4">umami-4</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Check for "natural flavor"</strong> - Ingredients labeled "natural flavor" may contain MSG or hidden glutamate <sup><a href="#src-natural">natural</a>, <a href="#src-natural-2">natural-2</a>, <a href="#src-natural-3">natural-3</a>, <a href="#src-natural-4">natural-4</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>
